WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.980
The oxygen in your next breath is, in part, made of leftovers.

2
00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:07.220
What it was left over from is the story.

3
00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:10.280
Rewind about 3 billion years.

4
00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:15.640
Watch the gauge. The air holds almost no oxygen.

5
00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:18.320
Something had to fill this sky.

6
00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:20.880
And something held it back.

7
00:00:24.283 --> 00:00:27.943
Nobody saw this world, so we rebuild it from ancient rocks.

8
00:00:29.263 --> 00:00:30.683
The sea held dissolved iron.

9
00:00:31.943 --> 00:00:33.743
That iron had electrons to give.

10
00:00:34.923 --> 00:00:38.963
But with little oxygen to take them, much of that iron stayed dissolved.

11
00:00:40.117 --> 00:00:43.257
Life was already here, and all of it was microbial.

12
00:00:44.817 --> 00:00:50.077
Many of these cells likely made energy without oxygen, by fermenting or by making

13
00:00:50.077 --> 00:00:52.697
methane. Remember them.

14
00:00:53.877 --> 00:00:55.337
They come back near the end.

15
00:00:56.317 --> 00:00:57.717
Then came a new trick.

16
00:00:59.077 --> 00:01:00.777
Water holds its electrons tightly.

17
00:01:02.357 --> 00:01:05.417
Cyanobacteria learned to pull them out, powered by sunlight.

18
00:01:06.737 --> 00:01:09.117
They kept the electrons to build their own food.

19
00:01:10.357 --> 00:01:12.977
The waste was oxygen gas, and they let it go.

20
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:17.120
In shallow seas, these cells spread as sticky mats.

21
00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:19.400
So here is a puzzle.

22
00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:22.320
The mats were releasing oxygen.

23
00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:25.680
Why did the air not fill with it right away?

24
00:01:31.683 --> 00:01:34.783
Because oxygen had many other places to go.

25
00:01:35.883 --> 00:01:37.843
Oxygen strongly pulls in electrons.

26
00:01:39.283 --> 00:01:41.183
Each oxygen molecule can take four.

27
00:01:42.323 --> 00:01:44.963
And the sea held iron with electrons to give.

28
00:01:46.203 --> 00:01:51.223
So iron handed its electrons to oxygen, formed rust-like minerals, and sank.

29
00:01:52.283 --> 00:01:56.423
Layer by layer, iron and silica built striped rock on the sea floor.

30
00:01:57.703 --> 00:02:02.523
These are banded iron formations, and much of the iron we mine today comes from

31
00:02:02.523 --> 00:02:08.683
them. Some layers may be the work of microbes that rust iron without any oxygen.

32
00:02:09.833 --> 00:02:11.773
Iron was not the only taker.

33
00:02:13.193 --> 00:02:18.053
Volcanic gases, methane, and minerals on land all consumed oxygen too.

34
00:02:19.373 --> 00:02:21.333
As an analogy, picture a bathtub.

35
00:02:22.593 --> 00:02:23.613
The mats are the tap.

36
00:02:24.893 --> 00:02:27.413
Every one of those takers is an open drain.

37
00:02:28.533 --> 00:02:30.633
Here is the idea to hold on to.

38
00:02:31.913 --> 00:02:34.433
Making oxygen is only half of the story.

39
00:02:35.733 --> 00:02:38.873
The air keeps only the oxygen that nothing else takes first.

40
00:02:40.067 --> 00:02:41.747
So when did the tap turn on?

41
00:02:43.267 --> 00:02:47.407
Cyanobacteria most likely arose well before oxygen built up.

42
00:02:48.447 --> 00:02:49.787
By how long is debated.

43
00:02:51.147 --> 00:02:54.807
Some rocks hint at brief whiffs of oxygen before the lasting rise.

44
00:02:55.917 --> 00:02:57.317
Then came the change.

45
00:02:58.597 --> 00:03:04.897
Between about 2.4 and 2.1 billion years ago, oxygen rose, wavered, and finally

46
00:03:04.897 --> 00:03:10.337
stayed. In bathtub terms, more was now arriving than the drains could take.

47
00:03:11.537 --> 00:03:13.737
Why the balance tipped then is still debated.

48
00:03:15.217 --> 00:03:17.777
Geologists call this the Great Oxygenation Event.

49
00:03:18.817 --> 00:03:20.737
But the tub was nowhere near full.

50
00:03:22.097 --> 00:03:25.537
Oxygen stayed far below today's level for a very long time.

51
00:03:26.550 --> 00:03:28.410
Now remember the old microbes.

52
00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:35.650
An oxygen molecule that is only partly filled with electrons can damage DNA and

53
00:03:35.650 --> 00:03:40.370
proteins. For many of those microbes, oxygen was toxic.

54
00:03:41.650 --> 00:03:42.770
So what happened to them?

55
00:03:43.970 --> 00:03:45.230
Was there a mass die-off?

56
00:03:51.167 --> 00:03:54.947
A mass die-off is often claimed, but the evidence is thin.

57
00:03:56.527 --> 00:03:59.707
Microbes that live without oxygen are called anaerobes.

58
00:04:00.727 --> 00:04:04.767
They clearly survived, in mud, deep water and other airless places.

59
00:04:06.087 --> 00:04:08.667
They thrive there still, including in your own gut.

60
00:04:09.717 --> 00:04:13.737
The oxygen that was left over did not end the old world of life.

61
00:04:14.977 --> 00:04:16.337
It pushed it into the shadows.

62
00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:19.080
Now run the clock forward.

63
00:04:20.420 --> 00:04:22.920
Oxygen stayed low for well over a billion years.

64
00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:27.900
Then, much later, it rose again, toward the level you breathe today.

65
00:04:29.140 --> 00:04:30.300
Take that breath again.

66
00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:32.620
It began as waste.

67
00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:37.580
And what reaches your lungs is what the rest of the planet left behind.
